I started making RingSimV1 a bit after my first version of my N-Body sim.
I used NumPy to compute the major Body-particle forces quickly, and I was satisfied with its speed.
 I added features that I felt like adding, and it started to turn into this little sandbox game. 
I started with adding bodies you can create and throw at the ring system. I then added randomized generation for rings and moons. 
A while later, after discovering Taichi, I realized there’s a lot more to ring systems.
Particle collisions, and even the tiny force of gravity between ring particles, affected entire ring systems.
The timescales real rings evolved at was also a problem. Verlet integration isn’t very stable with high delta time.
For this new project for more accurate rings, I used a Wisdom Holman integrator.
In essence, WH integration finds the 2-body orbit of a particle around the central body (like the sun, or in this case Saturn) and moves it analytically along that orbit.
This makes WH integration extremely stable with very large timesteps.
That said, doing this integration efficiently is… a pain to say the least. 
This was due to the fact that you need to get the orbit of every particle from just its position and velocity.
I then used statistical collisions in radial/azimuth bins. 
These bins basically divide space into rings and then divide those rings into segments.
Then, using the density, I would do statistical collisions.
The collisions didn’t work very well, but the visible resonances you would see forming over long durations were satisfying.
I took a long break from rings to work on my parallel Barnes Hut N-Body sim after this, but I came back for the game jam in my AP Computer Science Principles class.
The theme was “the sun, the moon, the stars,” so it was a perfect fit.
I remade my RingSimV1 with Taichi, making it able to handle tens of millions of particles without breaking a sweat.
I was just a little too late to add the velocity-based color visualizer which really made the visuals so much better, but it still got the “most ambitious” award from my teacher, so I was happy.
I then added a version with collisions and N-Body, patching in the Barnes Hut N-Body sim and reusing the quadtree to find collision candidates. 
It worked okay, but it was pretty slow and the clumps of particles were a bit awkward.
Since then, I've only really worked on optimizing and adding more features to my Barnes Hut N-Body sim, but maybe I'll come back to this and make an even more accurate simulator.
